course: dictionary
lesson: 3832
title: Fort: a stronghold, a hideout, or looking after things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fort is a strong building, or group of buildings, with thick walls, built to protect people from attack. Context clues can help you spot this meaning. In «The Roman soldiers lived in a fort with thick walls and towers», the walls and towers tell you a fort is a place built for safety.
explain: Fort can also mean a children's den made from boxes, blankets or branches. Read «Mia built a fort from blankets and two chairs» and ask what fits: blankets and chairs make a hideout, not a stone castle. When you are not sure, swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Hold the fort» is a phrase with a different meaning. If Dad says, «Can you hold the fort while I pop to the shop?», he does not want you to build walls. He wants you to look after things while he is away. Words that come together like this are called phrases, and you learn them by meeting them in context.
fact: A fort is a strong building or group of buildings with thick walls, built to protect people from attack.
fact: «Fort» can also mean a den or hideout made from boxes, blankets or branches.
fact: Roman forts, like those along Hadrian's Wall, had walls, towers, gates and ditches.
fact: «Hold the fort» means look after things while someone is away.
fact: Safety first: «Holes in sand deeper than your knees» can fall in and bury a child, so never dig into sand banks, and «snowploughs cannot see a fort in a snowbank by the road», so keep snow forts away from roads.
exercise: «The Roman soldiers lived in a fort with thick walls and towers.» What is a fort? | A soft bed made of blankets | A boat with sails | A type of snowball | *A strong building that protects people from attack
exercise: «Mia built a fort from blankets and two chairs.» What kind of fort is this? | *A children's den or hideout | A fort made of snow | A strong stone castle | A Roman fort with towers
exercise: Dad says, «Can you hold the fort while I pop to the shop?» What does he mean? | Lock the front gate forever | Stand guard with a sword | *Look after things while I am away | Build a den in the garden
test: «Tunnels in sand or snow can fall in and bury a child.» What should you avoid when building a fort outdoors? | Using a bucket and spade on flat grass | Building with an adult nearby | Making a snow fort in the back garden | *Digging tunnels into sand banks
test: Roman forts, like those along Hadrian's Wall, were built to protect people. What did they have? | *Walls, towers, gates and ditches | Trampolines and swings | Sails and anchors | Sofas and curtains
exam: In «They built a fort with thick walls to stop the attack», what does fort mean? | A den made from branches | A snowbank by the road | A way of looking after things | *A strong building to protect people
